x86_64 port — twins of arch/i386, not a second kernel tree
==========================================================

The portable code (kernel/, mm/, fs/, net/, most drivers/) stays one
copy. This directory is the CPU-specific half. Do not sprinkle
#ifdef __x86_64__ through kernel/syscall.c.

Makefile: ARCH=x86_64 selects this tree, x86_64-elf-gcc, a 64-bit
linker script, and qemu-system-x86_64 + OVMF.

First visible step (done): identity-mapped kernel at 1 MiB, COM1
hello from long mode (`make ARCH=x86_64 test-uefi`), and the same
image on a real UEFI PC (MSI Tiger Lake-H, GOP at 0x400000000).
Portable kernel/*.c is not linked yet.

Files to add here (same names as arch/i386/ unless noted):

  boot/efi/efi_main.c     stay in long mode; load a 64-bit ELF kernel
  boot/efi/efi_jump.asm   no 64→32 trampoline
  entry.asm               long mode, 4-level pages, _start
  linker.ld               higher-half, 4 KiB or 2 MiB pages
  gdt.c / isr.asm / interrupts.c
  paging.c                PML4, phys_addr_t in every PTE
  switch.asm              64-bit context switch
  sysentry.asm            syscall (not sysenter / int 0x80)
  uaccess.asm             copy_*_user for 64-bit canonical addresses
  include/memlayout.h     KERNEL_VBASE, direct map
  include/paging.h        64-bit PTE bits
  include/bootinfo.h      can keep the same BOOTINFO layout

User ABI is Linux x86_64 (syscall numbers from
arch/x86/entry/syscalls/syscall_64.tbl). That is a new table in
kernel/syscall.c keyed by the arch, or a table this directory
exports. i386 int 0x80 stays in arch/i386. Compat (running today's
Alpine i386 binaries on a 64-bit kernel) is later, not the first
hello.

First visible step (done): QEMU+OVMF and a real UEFI PC both show
"Hello world from MyOS!" from a 64-bit kernel. The x86_64 live
userland is built (`make ARCH=x86_64 live-uefi`, Chromium,
ELF64 myos-login). The kernel cannot exec it yet. Next: our own
page tables and a higher half, then musl x86_64 hello.
